import { BytesReaderCopy } from "../model"

pub fn copy -> BytesReaderCopy = BytesReaderCopy {
  summary: "std/bytes/base64 represents bytes as Base64 for a text-only field. Choose standard or URL-safe Base64 and use the decoder that matches that format.",
  taskTitle: "Carry a label’s bytes through a text field",
  purpose: "Suppose you want to turn the label café into UTF-8 bytes and put them in a text-only field. UTF-8 converts text into byte values. Base64 represents those bytes using letters, numbers, and a small set of other characters. It has standard and URL-safe forms.",
  typescriptBefore: "TypeScript’s TextEncoder turns café into a Uint8Array of bytes. length counts them, toHex() shows their contents, and toBase64() writes standard Base64. alphabet: \"base64url\" selects the URL-safe characters, and omitPadding: true leaves off trailing =. The final TextDecoder turns the original bytes back into UTF-8 text.",
  benefit: "Both languages combine existing conversion operations. Seseragi distinguishes String from immutable Bytes in the types, making the conversion from text to bytes and back to text explicit. A decoding failure is returned as Left, and success as Right.",
  setup: "The names bytes, text, hex, and base64 after import * as alias std/bytes, std/text, std/bytes/hex, and std/bytes/base64 respectively. text.encodeUtf8 turns text into bytes. base64.encode and base64.encodeUrl represent those same bytes in different formats.",
  after: [
    "let content = text.encodeUtf8 \"café\" names the conversion result content. A let name cannot be reassigned. A call places its argument after the function name, separated by a space. One byte holds a value from 0 through 255; café takes five bytes.",
    "The standard representation is Y2Fmw6k=; the URL-safe representation is Y2Fmw6k. The trailing = is padding that makes the standard representation’s length a multiple of four. This label does not use them, but standard + and / become - and _ in the URL-safe alphabet.",
    "fn decoded -> String defines a function with no arguments that returns a string; decoded () calls it. fn introduces the definition, and -> String states the result type. text.decodeUtf8 content returns Either: Left with a failure reason or Right with text.",
    "match chooses a branch for those two results. Left _ ignores the reason here and returns Rejected. Right value names the decoded string value and returns it. The example’s content is valid UTF-8, so the final line is café.",
    "A string enclosed in backticks inserts expression results with ${...}. The example inserts the byte count, hexadecimal representation, two Base64 representations, and original text into one string, then displays it with println.",
    "fatal: true makes TextDecoder throw on invalid UTF-8. A BOM is a marker that can use the U+FEFF character at the start of text. ignoreBOM: true keeps that character instead of removing it, matching Seseragi’s decoding policy.",
  ],
  details: [
    "encode/decode are the standard pair; encodeUrl/decodeUrl are the URL-safe pair. Standard Base64 includes the required = padding, while the URL-safe form has none. Choose the pair required by the receiving system.",
    "Decoding accepts only the one spelling its encoder would produce for those bytes. Whitespace, characters from the other alphabet, incorrect padding, and nonzero unused trailing bits are rejected. Base64DecodeError distinguishes the reasons.",
    "Decoding Base64 produces Bytes. To display them as text, separately use decodeUtf8 to check that they are UTF-8. The example’s decoded function rereads the original content directly; it does not decode the Base64 strings.",
    "Base64 does not encrypt, compress, or verify signatures. URL-safe does not guarantee that a destination or token is safe. These conversions create new values from the entire input; this small example does not process a large payload incrementally.",
  ],
  nodeNote: "The inspected Node 24 runtime lacks Uint8Array methods such as toHex()/toBase64(). With Node/Bun, import { Buffer } from \"node:buffer\" and write Buffer.from(content).toString(\"base64\") or toString(\"base64url\"). Buffer is a Uint8Array subclass with conversion methods. Buffer decoding is permissive; for strict acceptance, encode the decoded bytes again in the chosen format and require an exact match with the original input.",
}
